# Host tools

geniro's render and card tools (`report_findings`, `show_chart`, `show_metrics`, `show_comparison`, `show_gallery`, `show_artifact`, `artifact_answers`, `propose_patch`, `propose_plan`, `notify_user`, `publish_action`, `remove_action`, `monitor`, `stop_monitor`, `list_monitors`, `team_note`, `team_notes`, `record_outcome`, `goals`, `move_stage`, `read_teams`, `propose_team`; the shared memory's four tools are in `.claude/rules/daemon-memory.md`, the knowledge store's three in `.claude/rules/daemon-knowledge.md`). Layout and wiring: `mcp-tools.md`. Brokers live in `v1/agents/services/` over `HostSinkBroker`.

- `HostSinkBroker`: the disposer is identity-checked (per entry) and `deliver` never throws. Keep both in the base only. Per-sink facts go in `register`'s options (`answersAtOnce`) and leave with the sink; never keep a side set beside it.
- A chat's tools that hold nothing of the turn (`report_findings`, `show_chart`, `show_metrics`, `show_comparison`, `show_gallery`, `show_artifact`, `notify_user`, `publish_action` / `remove_action`, `record_outcome`, the monitor tools, and an analysis's own tools — `.claude/rules/daemon-thread-analysis.md`) stay registered past it (`ChatService.keepHostTools`, each row written on its own fork), because the CLI opens a stretch by itself when a background command reports. A team's stay between passes on the same terms. The next turn's registration replaces them; a start that failed and a deleted run drop them. A card that parks on the user (`ask_user_question`, `propose_plan`, `propose_patch`, `propose_team`, `save_project_instructions`) still dies with its turn, which sweeps it, and answers that no turn is running. The monitor tools stay only where the call is put to the user first (`hostToolCallUngatedReason` null), since the approval chip can move while no turn runs.
- The tool result is a receipt; the row is the only copy of the payload.
- A card is attributed to a delegate only when the turn is held, exactly one delegate is open, and its launching call is known (`attributableDelegate`). Otherwise null; never guess.
- Positional payloads (`show_chart` series vs `labels`, `show_comparison` cells vs `options`): blank an unreadable entry, never drop it, and re-align on both sides (renderer twin). A gallery is a set; drop unreadable entries.
- `show_metrics`: figures are preformatted strings (never coerce); sentiment is stated, and an unknown value reads as absent.
- `show_comparison`: recommendation is matched to an option by name (case- and whitespace-insensitive).
- `show_gallery` carries paths only; the daemon never opens a local one, and the renderer reads them through the image route's guards. An http(s) entry is downloaded by the daemon when the tool is called (`GalleryImageService` over `utils/remote-image.ts`, public hosts only — root `AGENTS.md` → *Constraints*, item 7), stored with the run's attachments, and the row names that file with the URL as `source`; a failed download drops that tile and the receipt names the URL and why. Outside `auto`, a call naming a URL is put to the user (`hostGalleryDownloads` in `autoApproves`, the between-turn approval included); a call that cannot be drawn or was cancelled deletes what it downloaded. `MAX_GALLERY_IMAGES` bounds reads and downloads per call.

- `show_artifact`:
  - Never `srcdoc`, except inside a share envelope (`v1/shares`), where the page is decrypted in the reader's browser under the same CSP as a meta tag. The frame's sandbox and CSP: root `AGENTS.md` → *Constraints*.
  - `share: true` (listed only while agents may share) shares AFTER the publish; a refused share is a `Not shared:` line in the receipt and never fails the publish. `ShareService.shareForAgent` re-checks the switch itself, because a kept CLI can hold a description listed while it was on.
  - Caps refuse, never truncate. A refused page image, audio or video file refuses the whole page.
  - Audio and video (`<audio>`, `<video>`, `<source>` `src`) are opened and judged before anything is written (`utils/artifact-page-media.ts` over `utils/artifact-media-policy.ts`, the picture's path rules through `openArtifactFile`), then streamed from that same descriptor into `media/<sha256>.<ext>` under the artifact — the one await inside a publish, so the version and the run's removal are read again after it. The served page points each at the media route under the page's key (`withMediaRoute`) and is served under `ARTIFACT_SERVED_PAGE_CSP` (`media-src 'self'`); a saved or shared copy keeps the stored name, keeps the portable `ARTIFACT_PAGE_CSP` and plays nothing.
  - `data-geniro-open` and the page's answers ride the frame wrapper, never the page runtime (which ships in saved files): `open-path` and `answer` messages, acted on by the host only while the frame holds focus, and `open-path` only on a fresh click (`navigator.userActivation`, one per click). Each `answer` carries the page's save count; the host hands the merged set back to the saving page with the counts it confirmed (`answers` with `confirmed`), so the runtime keeps only picks not yet confirmed over a set another frame saved, and a refused batch is sent again (`ANSWERS_RETRY_MS`) before the frame says it was not kept. A merge refuses (`ARTIFACT_ANSWERS_UNREADABLE`) rather than write over an answers file it cannot read. The answers are kept in `answers.json` beside the versions, each save MERGED into them (`ArtifactStoreService.mergeAnswers`: only the names a page changed, null forgets one, under `MAX_ARTIFACT_ANSWERS_BYTES`) so two frames of one page never undo each other, written through the token-gated chat routes, and read by the agent with `artifact_answers` (auto-approved, `isHostArtifactAnswersCall`; offered wherever `show_artifact` is).
  - `edits` + `base_version` revise a page without resending it (`ArtifactStoreService.resolvePatch`, `utils/artifact-edits.ts`): applied in order, all or nothing, to that version's source (else its served page); a base that is not the latest is refused, checked again in the write's synchronous section.
  - The serving key is registered with `registerSecret` at its mint site.
  - Team members publish too; publishers are dropped with the run, not at pass end.
  - Ids are the run's (`<runId>/<artifactId>`), whichever member publishes. A team page's row names the call its publishing conversation answers (`CallBroker.callAnsweredBy`, resolved once), and that call's envelope plus every ancestor call's still running lists it under `artifacts` (`notePublishedArtifact`, noted before the write so a call settling meanwhile still names it, undone if the row fails). The notes start from `CallBroker.callToNoteOn`: the answered call, else — a callee carrying on after its call settled — its conversation's newest call, so a page or button published then still reaches the running calls above. A page row never fetches its call as an anchor (`anchorNeedsOf` / `unplacedStructureOf`), since it is drawn outside the call's card. The tool's run-wide wording keys on `ArtifactBroker.inTeamRun`, never on call registration (a team of one registers no calls).
- `propose_patch`:
  - The call auto-approves; the Apply card shows even in `auto`. Caps refuse.
  - `applyHostPatch` writes only inside the run's cwd per `utils/write-containment.ts` (shared with codex `acceptEdits`): lexical and real-path, under both readings. It is rechecked at write time with `O_NOFOLLOW`; hard-linked targets are refused.
  - `old_string` must match exactly once. Filesystem errors go to the model as their `code` only (paths leave the machine).
- `propose_plan`: no `stale` outcome; a blank note is normalized to absent; caps truncate.
- `notify_user` writes no row; it publishes on `run_status`.

- Descriptions are audited as a set (`mcp-server.service.spec.ts`). `report_findings` asks to be preferred over claude's `ReportFindings`; `propose_plan` says to use either plan mode or this tool, never both.
- No team member registers a findings/chart/metrics/comparison/gallery/notify sink; `show_artifact` is the one render tool members get, and `publish_action` / `remove_action`, the monitor tools, the team-note tools, `record_outcome` and `move_stage` the other host tools; the shared memory's and the knowledge store's tools are offered on every endpoint (`.claude/rules/daemon-memory.md`, `.claude/rules/daemon-knowledge.md`).
